N2  - Cement-based materials are fundamental in the construction industry, and enhancing their properties is an ongoing challenge. The use of superabsorbent polymers (SAP) has gained significant attention as a possible way to improve the performance of cement-based materials due to their unique water-absorption and retention properties. This study investigates the multifaceted impact of kaolin intercalation-modified superabsorbent polymers (K-SAP) on the properties of cement mortar. The results show that K-SAP significantly affects the cement mortar's rheological behavior, with distinct phases of water absorption and release, leading to changes in workability over time. Furthermore, K-SAP alters the hydration kinetics, delaying the exothermic peak of hydration and subsequently modifying the heat release kinetics. Notably, K-SAP effectively maintains a higher internal relative humidity within the mortar, reducing the autogenous shrinkage behavior. Moreover, K-SAP can have a beneficial effect on pore structure and this can be ascribed to the internal curing effect of released water from K-SAP. © (2024), (Tech Science Press). All Rights Reserved.
